# context prints the format, layers, registry, and rules for an agent.
cd $EXAMPLES/refund
exits 0 specval context
cmp stdout $WORK/refund.txt
! stderr .

# With an empty registry, context says so.
cd $WORK/empty
exits 0 specval context
stdout '^## Registered services\n\(none\)$'

# context lists a registered service that has no valid layer on its own line.
# The registry rules report that service.
cd $WORK/numberlayer
exits 0 specval context
stdout '^- manager: OrderManager$'
stdout '^- no valid layer: NumberEngine, StringAccessor$'

# Without a .specval.yaml, context exits 2.
cd $WORK
exits 2 specval context
stderr '^specval: no .specval.yaml in '

-- empty/.specval.yaml --
-- numberlayer/.specval.yaml --
-- numberlayer/.specval/data/services.yaml --
services:
  OrderManager: { layer: manager }
  NumberEngine: { layer: 7 }
  StringAccessor: accessor
-- refund.txt --
# Spec rules (specval)

Each change needs a spec at specs/<id>.yaml, and the spec must pass before you write code. To make a new spec, run `specval new <id>`. To check a spec, run `specval check <file>`. Each finding has a fix.

## Format
```yaml
specval: 1
id: ORD-142                # the ticket ID; the file is specs/<id>.yaml
kind: feature              # feature, bugfix, or chore
title: Customer can request a refund
summary: One paragraph that tells the problem and the new behavior.
acceptance:                # criteria that you can test, with the IDs AC1, AC2, ...
  - { id: AC1, text: "Given a delivered order, when the customer requests a refund, then ..." }
services:
  new:                     # the services that this change adds
    - { name: RefundEngine, layer: engine, purpose: Calculates refund amounts }
  changed: [OrderManager]  # the registered services whose behavior changes
calls:                     # each call between services that the change adds or changes
  - { from: OrderManager, to: RefundEngine, op: CalculateRefund, covers: [AC1] }
out_of_scope: [Partial refunds]
```

## Layers
Each layer can call only the layers after its arrow.
- manager -> engine, accessor
- engine -> accessor
- accessor -> resource
- resource -> nothing
The name of each service ends with the name of its layer: Manager, Engine, Accessor, Resource.

## Registered services
- manager: OrderManager
- engine: PricingEngine
- accessor: CatalogAccessor, OrderAccessor

## Rules
- ORD001 (error) Only OrderManager calls PricingEngine: One service controls pricing, so that all discounts apply in the same way.
- SV001 (error) Spec matches the specval format: The spec has the fields, the field names, and the value types that the schema (`specval schema`) gives. The other rules run only after this rule passes.
- SV101 (error) Calls use known services: The `from` and the `to` of each call is a registered service or a service in `services.new`.
- SV102 (error) Calls follow the layer rules: A service can call only the layers that its layer can call (see Layers).
- SV103 (error) New services are named for their layer: The name of a new service ends with the suffix of its layer (Manager, Engine, Accessor, or Resource).
- SV104 (error) New services are actually new: A service that is in the registry goes under `services.changed`, not under `services.new`.
- SV105 (error) New services use a known layer: The `layer` of a new service is one of the DPL layers.
- SV106 (error) New services are used: Each service in `services.new` is in one or more calls.
- SV107 (error) A call implements each acceptance criterion: The ID of each acceptance criterion is in the `covers` list of one or more calls. Thus each requirement shows the services that implement it.
- SV108 (error) Calls cover real acceptance criteria: Each ID in the `covers` list of a call is an acceptance criterion of the spec.
- SV109 (error) Changed services are registered: Each name in `services.changed` is in the registry.
- SV201 (warning) Changed services take part in a call: Each service in `services.changed` is in one or more calls.
